## 4. Simple regression (≈ 45 min)

Correlation is symmetric and unit-free. A regression gives a **slope in the units of the data**:

$$
\text{life\_exp}_i = \alpha + \beta \, \ln(\text{GDP pc}_i) + \varepsilon_i .
$$

OLS chooses $\alpha, \beta$ to minimise the sum of squared residuals $\sum_i \hat{\varepsilon}_i^2$. We use `fixest::feols()`, with heteroskedasticity-robust standard errors (`vcov = "hetero"`), which is the default choice in applied work.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
m1 <- feols(life_exp ~ log_gdp_pc, data = cs, vcov = "hetero")
summary(m1)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
OLS estimation, Dep. Var.: life_exp
Observations: 199
Standard-errors: Heteroskedasticity-robust 
            Estimate Std. Error  t value   Pr(>|t|)    
(Intercept) 15.73085   3.431312  4.58450 8.0746e-06 ***
log_gdp_pc   5.91600   0.340277 17.38583  < 2.2e-16 ***
---
Signif. codes:  0 '***' 0.001 '**' 0.01 '*' 0.05 '.' 0.1 ' ' 1
RMSE: 4.25738   Adj. R2: 0.713424
```


:::

```{.r .cell-code}
b1 <- coef(m1)[["log_gdp_pc"]]
```
:::


(We created `log_gdp_pc` beforehand; `feols(life_exp ~ log(gdp_pc_ppp))` would give the same result.)

**Reading the coefficient (log-level model).** $\hat\beta = 5.92$ years. Because GDP enters in logs:

- a **1% increase** in GDP per capita is associated with $\hat\beta / 100 \approx 0.059$ more years of life expectancy;
- a **10% increase** is associated with about $\hat\beta \times \ln(1.1) = \hat\beta \times 0.0953 \approx 0.56$ years;
- **doubling** GDP per capita is associated with $\hat\beta \times \ln 2 \approx 4.1$ years.

The intercept (15.7) is the predicted life expectancy at $\ln(\text{GDP pc}) = 0$, i.e. GDP pc of \$1: outside the data, so not interpretable.

**$R^2$** is the share of the variance of life expectancy "explained" by the line: here 0.71, which is also $r^2$ from Section 3. Note the quotation marks: *explained* means statistically accounted for, not caused.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(cs, aes(gdp_pc_ppp, life_exp)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.6, colour = pal_cours[["principal"]]) +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", formula = y ~ x, colour = pal_cours[["accent"]],
              fill = pal_cours[["accent"]], alpha = 0.15) +
  scale_x_log10(labels = label_dollar()) +
  labs(
    title = "Richer countries have longer life expectancy",
    x = "GDP per capita (2021 PPP $, log scale)",
    y = "Life expectancy at birth (years)",
    caption = paste0("Source: World Bank, WDI, 2019. N = ", n_cs, " countries.")
  )
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Life expectancy against GDP per capita (log scale), 2019. Line: OLS fit with 95% confidence band.](seance-4_files/figure-html/fig-reg-1.png){#fig-reg width=768}
:::
:::


With a log x-axis, the fitted relationship is a straight line, which is exactly the log-level model.

